  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThis morning's upper air analysis placed an upper-level ridge \nover the eastern U.S. with an upper-level trough located over the \nWest Coast. This ridge of high pressure has brought a continuation\nof dry and above normal temperatures to the Mid-South. Late \nmorning temperatures range from the middle 60s to middle 70s. \n\nThe aforementioned ridge will push east tonight into Saturday as a\nsouthern stream trough moves from the Southern Plains into the\nLower Mississippi Valley. This will gradually bring an end to the\nprolonged dry weather the Mid-South has experienced thus far. \n\nShort-term models and CAMs suggest rain will begin to develop\nacross the area on Saturday, mainly during the afternoon. Model \nsoundings indicate there will still be a substantial amount of dry\nair initially, limiting the amount and coverage of rain showers\nuntil better moisture arrives later Saturday night into Sunday.\nA few isolated thunderstorms are possible during this period as \nweak elevated instability will be present. Average total rainfall \namounts for the weekend are anticipated to range from less than a \nhalf inch west of the Mississippi River, and approaching one inch \nnear the Tennessee River. Mid level shortwave ridging will build \nback into the area Sunday night into Monday.\n\nLong range deterministic and ensemble model trends continue to\nshow a deeper upper-level trough moving through the Lower\nMississippi Valley next Tuesday into Wednesday. This next system\nwill bring another chance for showers and perhaps a few\nthunderstorms to the Mid-South. A low CAPE, high shear environment\nwill be present with confidence remaining low for severe \nthunderstorm potential as instability is expected to remain \nmeager at best. LREF probabilities for rainfall one inch or \ngreater remain between 20-30 percent for this time period.",
